the business of growing up is something  we normally think comes to a close when  we get a 16 or so and finally turn into  those fully finished products adults up  until then our growth this subject of  quite a lot of collective fascination  20th century psychology beginning with  the work of the Swiss clinician Jean  Piaget pioneered an approach to child  development that meticulously identified  and labeled every principal stage an  average infant might go through on the  developmental journey of its earliest  years thanks to this work we now know  that it's six months a child will be  able to sit up on its own pick up a  small object such as raisin using a  thumb and forefinger and recognize its  own image in a mirror though it will  most likely take another three months  before it can drink from a cup on its  own and understand simple requests by  two it will start to say I and you and  it will probably be able to put on a hat  by itself around four one can expect it  to use sentences several words long and  quite possibly invent an imaginary  friend an achievement that belongs to  what Piaget called the symbolic function  substage between the ages of four and  seven children can begin to grasp  abstract concepts like time and a  country but typically make mistakes  around the use of less than and more  than parents uncles aunts and  grandparents tend to be deeply  interested in these developmental  milestones which become the stuff of  family legend  however after about 16 the attention  society pays to the maturation of an  individual becomes ever more coarsely  grained the focus shifts to external  material matters we track what someone  gets in their university degree what job  they secure and how they progress up the  corporate hierarchy growing up become  synonymous with getting ahead it in  truth we never stopped having  opportunities to grow in an emotional  rather than material or physical way  perhaps between the ages of 19 and 21  without anyone really focusing on this  happening we might radically rethink our  view of how to handle our parents  shortcomings or of you  envy can take a leap forward in the  middle of our 23rd year or as we  approach 33 lying in bed early one  morning in a hotel we might amend our  sense of who's to blame for certain  relationship difficulties we may look  more or less the same but inside slow  unheralded moments of emotional growth  can be going on unfortunately we have  nothing like the clear detailed stages  to measure ourselves against the babies  and young children enjoy and that might  give us the encouragement we'd need to  note and foster stages of growth yet  every adult life contains a potential to  acquire new emotional skills each in its  own way of significant as a child  mastering a quirk of language in English  for instance saying I thought rather  than I thinked or learning to ride a  bicycle to develop emotionally involves  a range of steps learning to understand  and sympathize with oneself to take  proper stock of one's childhood  influences to communicate flaws and  eccentricities to others in good time to  interpret others beyond what they have  directly said to us to recognize the  hard edges of reality without being  destroyed by them to accept one's need  for consolation and assistance to  achieve a necessary degree of confidence  to know how to despair without wholly  giving up on existence we can imagine  plotting this kind of inner journey like  Islands each one of them marked by  settlements which we should take the  trouble  systematically to go in the visit as we  might the cities of Renaissance Italy or  the beauty spots on the Pacific Highway  we tell our friends excitedly that we'd  learnt about emotional translation or  how to achieve excellent grades in  communication we might throw a party  when we'd reached  cheerful despair despite misleading  external signs we aren't ever in fact  done with a tricky business of becoming  that hugely important 